# Subagent Delegation Policy

Subagents exist to preserve context and isolate noisy work.

- Delegate when the task is long-running, verbose, investigative, or naturally
  parallel.
- Keep subagents narrow. Prefer 2-4 focused subagents over a large catalog of
  vague helpers.
- Write descriptions that clearly say when the subagent should be used.
- Return structured summaries instead of raw logs.

## Anti-patterns

- Do not create generic "helper" subagents.
- Do not use subagents for one-shot work that a skill or direct tool call can
  handle cleanly.
- Do not copy large amounts of parent context when a concise brief is enough.
